{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.BigOperators.Group.Finset","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":12,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling.selected_lam_floor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling.selected_lam_floor","kind":"theorem","line":222,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/PenaltyFamilyCeiling.lean#L222-L228","doc":"And the *selected* `lam` is far weaker still: at `lam* = 0.010434072` the depth is\n`26 * lam* / 4 = 0.0678214682`, so that program's floor is `3.20925948 = 1.4859x`.  `lam`'s\nentire contribution — already exercised, already inside the measured `1.56363x` — is bounded by\n`0.068`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling.budget_needed_for_cut_32","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling.budget_needed_for_cut_32","kind":"theorem","line":209,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/PenaltyFamilyCeiling.lean#L209-L220","doc":"The falsifiable form, and the only thing that reopens the route: for the floor to drop to\nthe `cut <= 32` requirement the per-row depth `lam / 4` must satisfy\n`26 * lam / 4 ≥ 3.27708095409687544 - 2.5643604628939807681`, hence `lam ≥ 0.1096`.  The probe\ncan justify `lam < ln q0 / 2 = 0.05218`, so the route needs a curvature budget `2.1x` larger\nthan the one it has.  `lam_max` is a *sufficient* concavity condition there, not a measured\nbudget — `G` carries extra negative curvature from its gain functions — so exhibiting a larger\nadmissible budget is a live and well-posed question.  Nothing here forbids it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling.floor_at_measured_budget_exceeds_cut32","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling.floor_at_measured_budget_exceeds_cut32","kind":"theorem","line":188,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/PenaltyFamilyCeiling.lean#L188-L207","doc":"**The budget was measured, and it does not reopen the route.**  `ln q0 / 2` is only a\nsufficient concavity condition in the probe; the true budget is `-lam_max(H_G)/2`, since\n`G + lam * sum (u^2 - u)` is concave exactly when `H_G + 2 lam I <= 0`.  Measured over the\nHessian of the probe's own gradient at `mp.dps = 60`\n([`rev_e_infinity_hessian_budget_probe.py`](computations/deltalambda/rev_e_infinity_hessian_budget_probe.py)),\nthe tightest sampled value is `0.06439550031177121` at `pe = 0`, `T = JPAD = 26` —\n`1.2343x` the probe's limit, against the `2.1018x` `budget_needed_for_cut_32` demands.\n\nThe floor at that budget is `3.27708095409687544 - 26 * 0.06439550031177121 / 4 =\n2.858510202 = 1.3235x`, still above the `1.1873x` the arc needs, by `0.294149739`.  Since a\nsampled point is an *upper* bound on a budget that is an infimum over the domain, and the\nmeasured budget decreases with the row count and is still decreasing at `T = JPAD`, this\n**closes** the reopening condition rather than merely failing to meet it.\n\nTrap recorded with the measurement: the **diagonal** of `H_G` reads `lam <= 0.413049687\n= 7.9173x`, which looks like a decisive reopening.  `lam_max(H_G) >= max_i H_ii`, so that is\nalso only an upper bound — and the cross terms (the pair gains) inflate it by `6.41x`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling.floor_exceeds_cut32_requirement","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling.floor_exceeds_cut32_requirement","kind":"theorem","line":175,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/PenaltyFamilyCeiling.lean#L175-L186","doc":"**The floor, with the probe's own numbers, exceeds what the arc needs.**  The `lam = 0`\nascent at `pe = 6` measured `sup G ≥ 3.27708095409687544` (KKT residual `9.4e-14`), and at the\nconcavity limit `lam_max = ln q0 / 2 = 0.0521703601597597...` over the ascent's `J = 26` rows\nthe deepest admissible separable penalty is `26 * lam_max / 4 = 0.33910734...`.  So every bound\nthis family can certify is at least `2.93797361 = 1.3603x` the enumerated maximum, while\n`cut <= 32` needs `2.56436046289398077 = 1.1873x`.\n\nThe family is therefore **excluded**, not merely short — and excluded with the box charge set to\nzero, so the padding lead cannot rescue it either. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling.certified_floor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling.certified_floor","kind":"theorem","line":159,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/PenaltyFamilyCeiling.lean#L159-L173","doc":"**A FLOOR, not merely a shortfall: no admissible penalty can certify below\n`sup G - M`.**  If the unpenalised objective is known to reach `S` somewhere, and the penalty\nnever dips below `-M`, then every bound valid for the penalised objective is at least `S - M`.\nThis is the sharp form of the cap and the one the numeric theorems below use.\n\nWhy it matters that this is a floor rather than a shortfall: a *shortfall* statement compares\nthe cap against the overshoot of the measured certificate, which bundles the box charge in with\nthe relaxation gap and so understates the obstruction.  The floor is charge-free — it binds even\nif the padded charge were driven to zero. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling.sawtooth_le_quadratic_of_one_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling.sawtooth_le_quadratic_of_one_le","kind":"theorem","line":144,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/PenaltyFamilyCeiling.lean#L144-L157","doc":"A sawtooth penalty — one that vanishes at every non-negative integer — is never worse than\nthe quadratic one past the first cell, because it stays non-positive there while the quadratic\nhas gone non-negative.  Stated on the fractional part `t ∈ [0, 1]` of `u ≥ 1`.\n\nTogether with the fact that the two **coincide** on `[0, 1]` (both are `lam * u * (u - 1)`\nthere), this says the sawtooth dominates the quadratic pointwise — so it is the right member of\nthe family to take, and it still obeys the same `-lam/4` depth, hence the same cap. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling.quadratic_nonneg_of_one_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling.quadratic_nonneg_of_one_le","kind":"theorem","line":137,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/PenaltyFamilyCeiling.lean#L137-L142","doc":"The quadratic penalty is non-negative from `1` on, so beyond the first cell it **loosens**\nthe bound rather than tightening it.  This is what the sawtooth form avoids. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling.unit_penalty_eq_neg_quarter","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling.unit_penalty_eq_neg_quarter","kind":"theorem","line":132,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/PenaltyFamilyCeiling.lean#L132-L135","doc":"The depth is attained: at the half-integer the penalty is exactly `-lam/4`.  Without this\nthe bound above could be vacuously loose, and the cap would not be the right number. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling.unit_penalty_ge_neg_quarter","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling.unit_penalty_ge_neg_quarter","kind":"theorem","line":118,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/PenaltyFamilyCeiling.lean#L118-L130","doc":"**The deepest an admissible per-coordinate penalty can dip is `-lam/4`.**  For `lam ≥ 0`,\n`lam * t * (t - 1) ≥ -lam/4`, with equality at `t = 1/2`.  The constant is `1/4` and not\nsomething larger because the penalty must vanish at both ends of the unit cell: it is zero at\n`t = 0` and `t = 1` (validity at integer points), and its curvature is `2 lam` (the concavity\nbudget), which fixes the depth.\n\n**No restriction on `t` is needed**, and that is worth stating rather than hiding behind a\n`0 ≤ t ≤ 1` hypothesis the proof does not use: the inequality is `lam * (t - 1/2)^2 ≥ 0`, so it\nholds for every real `t`.  The depth bound is therefore a property of the *cell shape* alone and\ntransfers to every cell of a sawtooth penalty without a per-cell argument. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling.sum_penalty_ge","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling.sum_penalty_ge","kind":"theorem","line":108,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/PenaltyFamilyCeiling.lean#L108-L116","doc":"A separable penalty bounded below by `-d` on each coordinate is bounded below by\n`-(card * d)` in total.  This is the step that makes the cap scale with the number of rows the\nascent actually carries — and no further, since a padded row sits at `u = 0`, where every\npenalty in the family vanishes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling.drop_le_of_bounded_penalty","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling.drop_le_of_bounded_penalty","kind":"theorem","line":96,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/PenaltyFamilyCeiling.lean#L96-L106","doc":"**A bounded penalty buys at most its own depth.**  If `B` bounds the penalised objective\n`f + h` everywhere and the penalty never dips below `-M`, then `B + M` bounds `f`.  Read the\nother way: a certificate on `f + h` cannot be better than a certificate on `f` by more than\n`M`, so `M` caps what the penalty is worth. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling.concave_penalty_useless","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.PenaltyFamilyCeiling.concave_penalty_useless","kind":"theorem","line":76,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/PenaltyFamilyCeiling.lean#L76-L94","doc":"**A concave penalty is useless, which is why an admissible one must be convex somewhere.**\nIf `p` is concave and non-negative at the two ends of a cell, it is non-negative throughout —\nso a penalty that is concave on `[0, ∞)` and valid at every non-negative integer satisfies\n`p ≥ 0` everywhere, and `G + p ≥ G` makes the bound *worse* than the plain relaxation.\n\nThis is the structural reason the admissible family is shaped the way it is: a useful penalty\nmust have a convex region (so that it can dip below zero strictly inside a cell while vanishing\nat the cell's integer ends), and its curvature is then capped by `G`'s own concavity.  The cap\nis what `unit_penalty_ge_neg_quarter` turns into a depth, and the depth is what\n`certified_floor` turns into an exclusion. ","signatureChunk":0}]}